  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA surface cold front is expected to drift sewd tonight, reaching\nLake Erie/northern Ohio Wednesday morning. A few showers are\npossible, mainly across NW PA late tonight into Wednesday\nmorning with convergence ahead of the front. \n\nRecent model data has trended toward a more rapid\nweakening/southward shift of a ridge across the Upper Ohio\nValley region, which results in a faster arrival of a cold front\non Wednesday. This front should drift southward to central\nOhio/west central PA by late Wednesday afternoon. Increasing\nmoisture and a weak mid level shortwave trough is also expected\nto approach the region at the same time, sparking scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms. 0-6km shear is progged to remain at\nor below 20kt, though ML CAPE of 1000-1500 j/kg and some DCAPE\n(500-700 j/kg) could result in a gusty wind potential in some of\nthe storms. In addition, PWATS increase 1.8 to near 2.0 mainly\nnorth of I-70 by late in the day, resulting in a locally heavy\nrain/flash flood potential. The extent of the storm coverage is\nstill uncertain, though recent trends do show some increased\npotential for the heavy rain/strong storm threat late Wednesday.\n\nThe intensity of any showers/storms should decrease Wednesday\nnight with waning instability, though with the front in the area\nand another approaching shortwave scattered showers/isolated \nstorms are still expected.\n\nThe flow should back aloft Wednesday night/Thursday morning,\nresulting in the front stalling across the region. This wave\nshould track across the Upper Ohio Valley region Thursday, with\nadditional showers and thunderstorms expected. WNW flow aloft\nbehind the wave should push the front through the area Thursday\nafternoon and evening. Diurnal instability and continued high \nPWATS will result in mainly a locally heavy rainfall potential \nthrough the day. The showers and storms should gradually end by \nThursday night as the front exits, with dry weather returning \nfor Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate a WNW flow aloft across the region this\nweekend into early next week, as a surface front approaches and\nstalls across the Upper Ohio Valley region. Multiple waves\nembedded in the flow will result in periodic showers and\nthunderstorms across the region, especially late Saturday into\nMonday. Uncertainty remains in how long this pattern will\npersist, with some guidance indicating some improvement toward\nmid week.",
